-- This is a DUMMY FILE used for making LuaDoc for built-in functions in the os table.

--- Extends Lua's `os` library to provide process spawning capabilities.
-- @module os

--- Spawns an interactive child process *cmd* in a separate thread, returning a handle to that
-- process.
-- On Windows, *cmd* is passed to `cmd.exe`: `%COMSPEC% /c [cmd]`.
-- @param cmd A command line string that contains the program's name followed by arguments to
--	pass to it. `$PATH` or `%PATH%` is searched for program names.
-- @param[opt] cwd Optional current working directory (cwd) for the child process. When omitted,
--	the Textadept's cwd is used.
-- @param[opt] env Optional map of environment variables for the child process. When omitted,
--	Textadept's environment is used. In order to create a new environment that inherits
--	from Textadept's, you can: spawn `env` on macOS and Linux/BSD, or `set` on Windows;
--	iterate over output matches of "key=value" pairs (one per line), assigning them to the
--	new environment table; and finally use that table here.
-- @param[opt] stdout_cb Optional Lua function that accepts a string parameter for a chunk of
--	standard output read from the child. Stdout is read asynchronously in 1KB or 0.5KB chunks
--	(depending on the platform), or however much data is available at the time.
-- @param[optchain] stderr_cb Optional Lua function that accepts a string parameter for a chunk
--	of standard error read from the child. Stderr is read asynchronously in 1KB or 0.5kB
--	chunks (depending on the platform), or however much data is available at the time.
-- @param[optchain] exit_cb Optional Lua function that to call when the child process finishes. The
--	child's exit status is passed.
-- @return proc or `nil` plus an error message on failure
-- @usage os.spawn('lua ' .. buffer.filename, print) -- non-interactive
-- @usage local proc = os.spawn('lua -e "print(io.read())"', print) -- interactive
--	proc:write('foo\n')
--	proc:close() -- close stdin, not the process
-- @function spawn

--- A spawned process.
-- @type proc

--- Returns the status of the process, either "running" or "terminated".
function proc:status() end

--- Blocks until the process finishes (if it has not already done so) and returns its status code.
function proc:wait() end

--- Reads and returns stdout from the process, according to string format or number *arg*.
-- Similar to Lua's `io.read()` and blocks for input. The process must still be running. If an
-- error occurs while reading, returns `nil`, an error code, and an error message.
-- Ensure any read operations read all stdout available, as the stdout callback function passed
-- to `os.spawn()` will not be called until the stdout buffer is clear.
-- @param[opt='l'] arg Optional argument similar to those in Lua's `io.read()`. The default is
--	to read a line.
function proc:read(arg) end

--- Writes the given string input to the stdin of the process.
--
-- Note: On Linux when using the GTK or terminal version, if more than 65536 bytes (64K) are
-- to be written, it is possible those bytes need to be written in 65536-byte (64K) chunks,
-- or the process may not receive all input. However, it is also possible that there is a limit
-- on how many bytes can be written in a short period of time, perhaps 196608 bytes (192K). The
-- Qt version does not appear to have this limitation.
-- @param ... Standard input for the process.
function proc:write(...) end

--- Closes standard input for the process, effectively sending an EOF (end of file) to it.
function proc:close() end

--- Kills the running process, or sends it Unix signal *signal*.
-- @param[opt=9] signal Optional Unix signal to send. The default is to kill the process
--	(`SIGKILL`).
function proc:kill() end
